# Large Project Navigation

## Objective

In large codebases, orient using structure and targeted search rather than reading everything — find the relevant module efficiently.

## Core Principles

- Start from project structure (folder layout, entry points, README) to orient before diving into files.
- Use targeted search (symbol/grep search) to find relevant code instead of reading files sequentially.
- Only deeply read the files actually relevant to the current task, not the whole surrounding module.
- Follow imports/references to find related code rather than guessing file locations.

## Requirements

- Use search (grep/symbol search) to locate the relevant function/module before reading full files.
- Read directory structure at a high level first, then narrow into the specific area needed.
- When a task spans multiple modules, map out which ones are actually relevant before opening each one.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Reading every file in a large directory to find one function that a targeted search would have located instantly.
- Guessing at file locations instead of searching for the actual symbol/reference.
- Re-reading unrelated modules 'just in case' when they aren't relevant to the current task.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Relevant files were located via targeted search/structure, not exhaustive reading.
✓ Only files actually relevant to the task were deeply read.
✓ Navigation approach scaled reasonably with project size.

## Golden Rule

In a large codebase, knowing where to look is more valuable than reading everything.
